id: 224
data: {"event": "run.cancelled", "run_id": "run_0c9cf3209b084fcaaf22001725b055b4", "timestamp": 1795520703.158875, "completed": false, "partial": false, "interrupted": true, "turn_exit_reason": "interrupted_during_api_call", "seq": 224}
# final {"object":"hermes.run","model":"hermes-agent","created_at":1795520695.537289,"pending_controls":[],"run_id":"run_0c9cf3209b084fcaaf22001725b055b4","status":"cancelled","session_id":"run_0c9cf3209b084fcaaf22001725b055b4","admission_id":"7862238f24994d8b8c6f63e1486d8357","execution_generation":1,"output":"At the edge of the harbor, where the city’s lights ended and the Pacific began, Mara Venn stood on the deck of her thirty-foot sloop and watched the last gull circle above the mast.\n\nShe had named the boat Otherwise.\n\nIt was an old name painted in fresh blue letters, and it suited her. Mara had spent most of her life going where she was expected to go: to school, to the university, to the hospital room where her father had died, to the small apartment where she had lived alone afterward. At forty-one, with a severance check, a patched mainsail, and no one waiting for her at the other side of the ocean, she had decided to go somewhere else.\n\nOtherwise was not a beautiful boat. Her hull was white once, now the color of old teeth. The cabin smelled of salt, cedar oil, and the faint sourness of wet rope. A brass compass lived under the sprayhood, its glass cracked in one corner. Her engine started only when it felt respected.\n\nBut she was sound. The surveyor had said so, and Mara had paid him","usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0,"total_tokens":0}}
